      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "48869d83-5e24-5e97-b0c5-027c70819458",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Medhatithi Gautama, Pāṇini, Gotama (Akṣapāda Gautama), Nagarjuna\n- Texts: Nyāya Sūtras\n- Schools and Concepts: Indian logic, anviksiki, Vaisheshika, Nyaya, atomism, inference, tetralemma, epistemology, metaphysics\n\nFacts:\n- The development of Indian logic dates back to the anviksiki of Medhatithi Gautama around the 7th century BCE.\n- The development of Indian logic includes the Sanskrit grammar rules of Pāṇini around the 5th century BCE.\n- The development of Indian logic includes the Vaisheshika school’s analysis of atomism around the 2nd century BCE.\n- The development of Indian logic includes Gotama’s analysis of inference around the 2nd century, and Gotama was the founder of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy.\n- The development of Indian logic includes Nagarjuna’s tetralemma around the 2nd century CE.\n- The Nyāya Sūtras is an ancient Indian Sanskrit text composed by Akṣapāda Gautama and is the foundational text of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy.\n- The Nyāya Sūtras consists of five books with two chapters in each book.\n- The Nyāya Sūtras contains a cumulative total of 528 aphoristic sutras about rules of reason, logic, epistemology, and metaphysics.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "cdbe6637-bd81-588c-b1c7-be5e04c9f4d5",
        "text": "Page: Indian logic. Sentence: The development of Indian logic dates back to the \"anviksiki\" of Medhatithi Gautama (c. 7th century BCE) the Sanskrit grammar rules of Pāṇini (c. 5th century BCE); the Vaisheshika school's analysis of atomism (c. 2nd century BCE); the analysis of inference by Gotama (c. 2nd century), founder of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy; and the tetralemma of Nagarjuna (c. 2nd century CE).\nPage: Nyāya Sūtras. Sentence: The Nyāya Sūtras is an ancient Indian Sanskrit text composed by Akṣapāda Gautama , and the foundational text of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy.\nPage: Nyāya Sūtras. Sentence: The text consists of five books, with two chapters in each book, with a cumulative total of 528 aphoristic sutras, about rules of reason, logic, epistemology and metaphysics.",
